sections.body:
**Answer:** The journal is a per-namespace (user or shared team folder) log of every file operation, in order. Each entry has a monotonically increasing LSN (log sequence number) and contains: event type (file_added / file_modified / file_deleted / file_moved / conflict), file_id, version, details (new parent, new name, etc.), timestamp. Devices maintain their `journal_pos` (last seen LSN) and pull deltas via `GET /journal/since?lsn=N`. The journal is **append-only** for three reasons: (1) **Write throughput**: appends don't contend with updates or deletes — a single MySQL shard can sustain 10 k appends/s vs ~1 k UPDATEs/s. (2) **Audit / history**: keeping the full history allows debugging ("when did this file get renamed?"), supports version restore, and provides evidence for legal/compliance. (3) **Multi-device catch-up**: an offline device can resume from any LSN — even after weeks offline, as long as the journal entries are retained (30 days free, 1 year Pro). To bound journal size, entries older than the retention period are archived to cold storage (S3 Glacier / Magic Pocket cold tier). The journal is the source of truth for the sync protocol; the `files` table is a materialized view derived from replaying the journal. If `files` and `journal` disagree, the journal wins. This is the event-sourcing pattern, applied to file sync. Trade-off: replaying the journal to derive state is slow for full reconstruction (used only on first-time device setup); the `files` table is the fast read path.
**Visual:**
```
Journal (append-only, per namespace ns=42):
  LSN=1  file_added     file=100 v1 {name:"doc.pdf", size:12MB}
  LSN=2  file_modified  file=100 v2 {size:13MB}
  LSN=3  file_added     file=101 v1 {name:"img.jpg"}
  LSN=4  file_moved     file=100    {new_parent:5}
  LSN=5  file_added     file=102 v1 {name:"doc.pdf (Conflicted Copy phone)"}
  ...

Device sync:
  phone: GET /journal/since?lsn=3  -> [LSN=4, LSN=5]
  phone: applies events to local SQLite, sets journal_pos=5
  phone: long-polls /journal/longpoll?cursor=5&timeout=30  (waits for LSN=6+)
```
